        "id": 24005
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-28T19:55:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "yes, it is the mess up that caller creates when attribute specified is not in attribute_mapping at [0] and [1]. I don\u0027t see any scenario where caller can specify attribute not in attribute_mapping but there is one such situation we need to consider in ldap configuration[2], where attribute value itself is None i.e. user_name_attribute \u003d None with ldap_attr_name \u003d \"name\".\n\nIn such situation, ldap query searches users/groups based on {user/group}_id_attribute and how do we process further? Do we ignore filtering by returning the result as it is now or raise an exception to correct the ldap configuration?\n\n\n[0]\nhttps://github.com/openstack/keystone/blob/master/keystone/identity/backends/ldap/core.py#L243\n\n[1]\nhttps://github.com/openstack/keystone/blob/master/keystone/identity/backends/ldap/core.py#L362\n\n[2]\n[ldap] \nurl \u003d ldap://9.9.9.9\nuser_name_attribute \u003d None\nuser_id_attribute \u003d uid\nuser_mail_attribute \u003d None",
      "parentUuid": "ff346bd7_9d581931",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1393,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 1393,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "c7b536d40fc7ee30ce54011fe0ff5a846eff3300",
